ing the digits correctly in the standard algorithm.\n\nWait, perhaps I should write it out properly:\n\n440\n\u00d7714\n--------\nFirst, multiply 440 by 4: 1760\nThen, multiply 440 by 1 (tens place), which is 440, but shifted one position left: 4400\nThen, multiply 440 by 7 (hundreds place), which is 3080, shifted two positions left: 308000\n\nNow, add them up:\n\n1760\n+4400\n+308000\n--------\nLet's add 1760 + 4400 first: 1760 + 4400 = 6160\nThen add 6160 + 308000: 6160 + 308000 = 314,160\n\nAh, okay, so I must have made a mistake in the standard algorithm earlier by not shifting correctly. So the correct total is 314,160.\n\nWait, but earlier when I did the standard algorithm, I got 3,125,760, which is way off. I think I must have multiplied 440 by 700 as 308,000, but then when adding, I might have added an extra zero somewhere. Let me check that again.\n\nWait, 440 \u00d7 700 is indeed 308,000. Then 440 \u00d7 10 is 4,400, and 440 \u00d7 4 is 1,760. Adding them together: 308,000 + 4,400 = 312,400; then 312,400 + 1,760 = 314,160. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo the correct product is 314,160.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 440 and 714 is calculated by breaking down 714 into its place values and multiplying each part by 440, then summing the results. \n\n1. Multiply 440 by 4 (ones place): 440 \u00d7 4 = 1,760\n2. Multiply 440 by 1 (tens place): 440 \u00d7 10 = 4,400\n3. Multiply 440 by 7 (hundreds place): 440 \u00d7 700 = 308,000\n\nAdding these together: 1,760 + 4,400 + 308,000 = 314,160\n\nSo, the final computed product is 314,160.\n\nAnswer: 314,160",
